.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-collection-run 1  "tapectl-collection-run " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-collection\-run \- Stage one batch of a collection and write it to one tape
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl collection run\fR [\fB\-\-batch\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] <\fB\-\-label\fR> [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-init\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-move\-to\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR] [\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-jobs\fR] [\fB\-\-full\-confirm\fR] [\fB\-\-full\-confirm\-first\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-aware\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fINAME\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stage one batch of a collection and write it to one tape
.PP
Stages every unit in the batch once, writes one session to the destination label, then releases the staging of each unit that copy brings to its copy floor (the larger of its policy\*(Aqs `min_copies` and the sum of its `copies_at` targets); a unit still short keeps its staging for the further copies. Ends with what the copy holds and the next copy\*(Aqs command. Runs one collection (unlike `sync`, `status` and `plan`, which sweep every configured collection), since a batch write is a real, one\-shot tape action.
.PP
A unit that spans cartridges is released slice by slice, each slice once it has its copies, and the run names the slices released and the slices kept for the next `volume write`.
.PP
With \-\-init the destination is initialized first, on the blank cartridge in the drive, as `volume write \-\-init` initializes it, and the batch is packed against the capacity the init records. With \-\-move\-to the tape is moved there once it is sealed and confirmed, as `volume write \-\-move\-to` moves it; a `volume resume` of an interrupted run keeps the move.
.PP
Exit status: `volume write`\*(Aqs, the worst outcome among its copies in the order 6 > 5 > 4 > 3 > 2 > 0 (6 quarantined, 5 aborted, 4 interrupted, 3 confirm inconclusive, 2 nothing written, 0 sealed and confirmed); 1 when every copy sealed but a unit was refused for its dotfile; 75 when the catalog was busy before anything was written.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-batch\fR \fI<BATCH>\fR [default: 0]
Which batch to run (0 = the first)

Numbered against THIS run\*(Aqs own budget — the destination volume\*(Aqs recorded capacity, not the drive\*(Aqs generation. This matches `collection plan`\*(Aqs numbering only when `plan` was run (`\-\-generation <GEN>`) for the generation that volume was actually initialised as; otherwise the batch reviewed in `plan` is not necessarily batch N here.
.TP
\fB\-\-label\fR \fI<LABEL>\fR
Destination volume label: exactly one, since a run writes one copy. tapectl drives no changer, so a second copy needs a human to swap cartridges, and a run has no point where that could happen: a second \-\-label is refused. The volume is already initialized on its own cartridge, or \-\-init initializes it first. For each further copy, swap in the next cartridge after this run finishes and run `tapectl volume write <NEW\-LABEL> \-\-init \-\-full\-confirm` against the same staged data
.TP
\fB\-\-init\fR
Initialize the destination first, on the blank cartridge in the drive

`volume init LABEL`, as `volume write \-\-init` runs it (a cartridge with no barcode takes the label as its barcode). The batch is then packed against the capacity the init records. If the run stops after the init, the volume stays initialized and the run says what to run next.
.TP
\fB\-\-move\-to\fR \fI<LOCATION>\fR
Once sealed and confirmed, move the tape to this location

The volume and its cartridge, as `volume write \-\-move\-to` moves them. A location that does not exist is refused before anything is staged.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The tape drive, by its by\-id path

Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured.
.TP
\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR
Hash every staged slice in full before the tape moves

As `volume write \-\-prewrite\-hash`, for every copy this run writes.
.TP
\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR \fI<FILL_CEILING>\fR
Fill each tape to at most this fraction: `0.99` or `99%`

For this command only. Overrides the drive\*(Aqs `fill_ceiling` (default 97%).
.TP
\fB\-\-jobs\fR \fI<JOBS>\fR
How many units to stage at once (1 to 16; default `[staging] jobs`)

As `stage create \-\-jobs`; `[staging] jobs` is itself 1 by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-full\-confirm\fR
Read every copy this run writes back in full after sealing it

As `volume write \-\-full\-confirm`.
.TP
\fB\-\-full\-confirm\-first\fR
Read the run\*(Aqs first copy back in full, the others quickly

The one full readback to have before the run releases staging. A run writes one copy today, so this reads back the same copy `\-\-full\-confirm` does.
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\-aware\fR
Number the batches as `collection plan \-\-policy\-aware` does

The units a write would help (too few copies, a required location with no copy) are packed first.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fINAME\fR]
The collection to run (default: the only one configured)

With several configured, name one.
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.SH EXTRA
EXAMPLES:
  See the batches first:
    tapectl collection plan

  The first copy of batch 0, onto the blank cartridge in the drive, then to its shelf:
    tapectl collection run photos \-\-label L6\-0001 \-\-init \-\-full\-confirm \-\-move\-to home

  The second copy of what that run staged, on the next blank cartridge:
    tapectl volume write L6\-0002 \-\-init \-\-full\-confirm \-\-move\-to offsite

  The next batch (batch 0 again: the units written are no longer pending):
    tapectl collection run photos \-\-label L6\-0003 \-\-init \-\-full\-confirm \-\-move\-to home
